    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d304">

                <pb facs="0408" n="398" sameAs="frus:frus1944v02/pg398"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.796/566: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-01T21:00:00+01:00">March 1, 1944—9
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 1—8:49 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1691. In connection with the proposed agenda for aviation discussions with the
                    British, it is suggested that consideration be given to including a provision on
                    the terms of sale and licensing of aircraft exported. We understand the British
                    are thinking about giving special inducements to foreign purchasers to buy
                    British airplanes. For example, it has been reported in the press that KLM has
                    ordered Tudors. While this is not generally believed, it may be that the
                    inducements have been sufficient to cause them to order a type of airplane of
                    which not even the prototype has been built.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
